# Fastlane deployment — one concept, four apps

These four apps deploy to Google Play and the App Store the same way. Each
keeps its own code, but the **lanes, options, checks and commands are one
contract**. A developer (or agent) who knows one app's deploy knows all four.

| App | Repo | Android package | iOS bundle ID |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| NuCatch | `flutter_projects/nucatch-with-bloc` | `bomnguyen.nucatch` | `bomnguyen.game.nucatch` |
| Ô Ăn Quan | `flutter_projects/o_an_quan` | `o.an.quan` | `quan.an.o` |
| Hanoi Tower (Hasto) | `flutter_projects/hanoi_tower` | `com.bomdata.hasto` | `calm.hasto.balance` |
| MindCompass | `flutter_projects/mind_compass` | `mind.com.pass` | `com.pass.mind` |

All four sign under Apple team `R23LP5MFKD`.

## The sync rule

- **A change to the contract is made in all four repos, in the same session**:
  a lane, an option, a check, a default, the CHANGELOG line format, or a fix to
  a bug the others share (e.g. a parsing or build-environment fix). Then run
  [Verify](#verify) in each repo.
- If one app genuinely can't follow the contract, record the difference in
  [Per-app differences](#per-app-differences) **in all four copies of this
  file** — never let an app drift silently.
- This file is **identical in every repo** (`.agents/rules/fastlane-deployment.mdc`).
  Edit it once, copy it to the other three.

## Lanes (every app, both platforms unless noted)

| Lane | Does | Builds | Uploads |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `check_env` | Credentials (checked online), signing, release notes | no | no |
| `check_release` | Can the store take this version? (the preflight below) | no | no |
| `deploy_staging` | Preflight → build → Play **internal** track / **TestFlight** | yes | yes |
| `deploy_production` | Preflight → build → Play **production** / **App Store Connect** (+ submit for review on iOS). Already on the store → promotes / finishes it instead, or does nothing if it's already submitted / live (behaviour 7) | only if not uploaded yet | yes |
| `deploy` | Alias of `deploy_production` | yes | yes |
| `submit_review` (iOS) | Submit an already-uploaded build — no rebuild; waits for Apple's processing, submits an "Add for Review" draft | no | submission only |
| `upload_screenshots` | Store screenshots only | no | media only |
| `distribute` | Pre-built binary → Firebase App Distribution | no | yes |

Run from `android/` or `ios/`: `(cd android && bundle exec fastlane <lane> …)`.

## Options (same names everywhere)

| Option | Lanes | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `build_name:` / `build_number:` | all version-aware lanes | Version to check / build. Default: the app's release source (see differences) |
| `environment:DEVELOPMENT\|PRODUCTION` | `check_release` | Which release to check (default PRODUCTION) |
| `update_screenshots:true\|false` | `deploy_production` | Also replace the store screenshots with the committed sets. Default `false` |
| `submit:true\|false` | iOS `deploy_production` | Submit for App Review after the upload |
| `auto_release:true` | iOS `deploy_production`, `submit_review` | Go live as soon as Apple approves (default: manual release) |
| `release_status:draft\|completed\|…` | Android `deploy_*` | Play release status |
| `validate_only:true` | Android `deploy_*` | Play checks the whole upload, then discards it — nothing is published |

## Behaviour every app guarantees

1. **Store preflight before any build** (`check_release` runs the same code),
   stopping with the fix when the store can't take the version:
   - **Google Play:** the version code must be higher than every code on every
     track (production, beta, alpha, internal); the version name must not be
     lower than production's.
   - **App Store:** the version must be higher than the live one; the build
     number must be new for that version; when submitting, no other version may
     be in review or awaiting release (Apple allows one).
2. **Build numbers are parsed base 10** (`"09"` → 9 — never `Integer("09")`,
   which is octal) and Play changelog files are named by the plain version code
   (`9.txt`).
3. **Builds run without Bundler's environment** (`Bundler.with_unbundled_env`),
   or CocoaPods fails under `bundle exec`.
4. **Release notes** ("What's new") go only to the locales the store listing
   has; Play ≤ 500 characters. On iOS the locales are read from App Store
   Connect at deploy time (the live version's, plus any the new version really
   fills in) — never from a hard-coded list, which would add an empty language
   and make App Review submission fail ("missing a required attribute"). The
   CHANGELOG brief's English lines go to every `en*` locale, the Vietnamese
   ones to `vi*`.
5. **Export compliance** is answered on iOS submission (from `Info.plist`'s
   `ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption`, or the app's `…_USES_NON_EXEMPT_ENCRYPTION`
   setting).
6. **No secret in a tracked or bundled file.** Store credentials live only in
   git-ignored env files; `.env` files bundled into the app (pubspec assets)
   never hold store keys.
7. **An already-uploaded version is promoted, not rebuilt.**
   `deploy_production` (and `check_release`, which reports it) looks the exact
   version up first:

   | On the store | iOS | Android |
   | --- | --- | --- |
   | Not uploaded | build → upload → submit | build → upload |
   | Uploaded, not submitted (TestFlight / test track) | set What's New → attach the build → submit (`submit:true`) | promote that release to production with What's new |
   | Added for review / draft, not sent | submit the "Add for Review" draft | roll the draft / halted production release out |
   | Already submitted / live | "nothing to deploy" — success, changes nothing | "nothing to deploy" — success |
   | Another version in review | stop (Apple allows one) | — |

   Only the **exact** production version is promoted: on iOS the same build
   number (`asc_build_number`: `08` = `8`); on Play the same version code
   **and** release name. A staging build (`1.1.12.1`, or build `15.1`) carries
   the development Firebase config and is never promoted — the lane stops and
   asks for a higher build number.
8. **iOS waits for Apple's processing** before attaching a build to a version
   (`wait_for_processed_build`, up to 60 minutes), and removes from the new
   version any language with no description that the live listing doesn't
   have, before submitting.

## CHANGELOG lines (every release block)

After the four `flutter build …` commands, each block lists:

```
#### Fastlane deploy for Testing (Development)

`(cd android && bundle exec fastlane deploy_staging build_name:{A_VER}.{T} build_number:{A_BN})`

`(cd ios && bundle exec fastlane deploy_staging build_name:{I_VER} build_number:{I_BN}.{T})`

#### Fastlane deploy for Production

`(cd android && bundle exec fastlane deploy_production build_name:{A_VER} build_number:{A_BN} update_screenshots:false)`

`(cd ios && bundle exec fastlane deploy_production build_name:{I_VER} build_number:{I_BN} submit:true update_screenshots:false)`
```

- The iOS production line **always** has `submit:true`.
- `update_screenshots:` is `false` unless the user asks to update screenshots
  with that release.
- The Android staging and production lines share one version code (`{A_BN}`),
  and Play accepts a code once: after `deploy_staging`, that version's
  `deploy_production` stops (behaviour 7 never promotes a staging build) —
  release production with a higher build number.
- Deploy only when the user explicitly asks — deploying publishes to the
  stores. Run `check_env`, then `check_release`, then (Android) a
  `validate_only:true` dry run, then the deploy; report a failed check instead
  of changing version numbers on your own.

## Per-app differences

These are deliberate; everything not listed here follows the contract —
including the credentials file (`.env.fastlane` at the repo root), the default
version and notes (the latest `CHANGELOG.md` block), Play production releasing
as `completed`, and iOS `submit` defaulting to `true`, in all four apps.

| | NuCatch | Ô Ăn Quan / Hanoi Tower | MindCompass |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Deploy helper | `tool/release/fastlane_common.rb` (`NucatchRelease`) | `tool/release/fastlane_common.rb` (`StoreRelease`, identical in both repos) | `tool/release/fastlane_common.rb` (`MindCompassRelease`) |
| App config in the build | bundles `env` / `env_dev` as `.env`, then restores it | bundles `.env` / `.env_dev` as `.env`, then restores it | `--dart-define-from-file=.env_release` (production only) |
| Firebase step | `npm run flutterfire_setup[_dev]` | `npm run flutterfire_setup[_dev]` | none — builds against its one `lib/firebase_options.dart` (`package.json` has no flutterfire scripts) |
| iOS signing | manual, profile `NuCatch_AppStore` | automatic | automatic |
| Store locales (iOS reads the real ones at deploy time; this is the fallback) | en-US, vi | Ô Ăn Quan: en-US · Hanoi Tower: en-GB, vi | en-US, vi |

## Verify

After any change, in **each** repo:

```bash
ruby -c android/fastlane/Fastfile ios/fastlane/Fastfile <the deploy helper>
(cd android && bundle exec fastlane lanes)   # every contract lane listed
(cd ios     && bundle exec fastlane lanes)
# with credentials in place (builds and uploads nothing):
(cd android && bundle exec fastlane check_env)
(cd android && bundle exec fastlane check_release)
(cd ios     && bundle exec fastlane check_env)
(cd ios     && bundle exec fastlane check_release)
```
